+# Why `BATCH=200` in `sync.sh`
+
+## Working theory: the binding constraint is time, not record count
+
+Per vendor support, a **30-second gateway timeout** sits in front of
+`/v2/ingest`, and a call that exceeds it is killed at the edge. That is the
+theory `BATCH=200` acts on: `BATCH` is a *duration* budget and the record count
+is only a proxy for it. The theory fits every observation we have and no
+observation contradicts it, but it was never confirmed — see the table below
+before treating it as settled.
+
+It is enough to settle one thing regardless. `/v2/ingest` documents a limit of
+1000 records per call, and that limit governs what the endpoint will *accept*,
+not what the gateway in front of it will *wait for*. Under any reading of the
+evidence, the documented 1000 is not permission to raise `BATCH` to 1000.
+
+Support also puts the account on a **shared tier**. If that is right, per-call
+latency varies with load we do not control, and the same batch size could pass at
+one hour and fail at another. Whether our failures actually behaved that way is
+not recorded either way.
+
+## What is established, and how firmly
+
+| Claim | Basis | Strength |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Every failed cron run in the month before the change had `BATCH=500` | Our own run history | Solid |
+| No failure in the four days since `BATCH=200` | Our own run history; cron fires every 10 min, so ~575 invocations | Solid, but see below |
+| Failures were bare `502` from the vendor edge, no body | Our own logs | Solid |
+| There is a 30-second gateway timeout in front of `/v2/ingest` | Raj, vendor support, on a call | Single verbal source, unverified |
+| We are on the shared tier | Same call | Single verbal source, unverified |
+| 500 records "usually" exceeds 30s on the shared tier | Same call, and hedged by him | Single verbal source, hedged |
+| 500 records *causes* the timeouts | Inferred from the above | **Not established** |
+
+Known gaps, so they are not rediscovered:
+
+- **Causation was never demonstrated.** `BATCH=500` was never reproduced after
+  the support call. The change from 500 to 200 was not a controlled test; nothing
+  rules out a concurrent change on the vendor's side over the same days.
+- **The 500-record failure rate is unknown.** "Every failure ran at 500" does not
+  say every run at 500 failed. If runs at 500 also succeeded, record count alone
+  does not determine the outcome and shared-tier load is part of the mechanism.
+- **No call duration has ever been measured.** The quantity the whole theory
+  turns on is the one number nobody has. The `502`s are consistent with a
+  30-second timeout but were never timed against one.
+- **200 is chosen headroom, not a measured ceiling.** No batch size between 200
+  and 500 was tried. The largest safe batch is unknown; it is somewhere above
+  200 and probably below 500.
+- **Four clean days is thinner than the invocation count suggests.** Queue depth
+  per run is not recorded, and an invocation with few pending rows exercises
+  nothing. The number of clean runs at a *full* 200-row batch is unknown.
+
+## Ruled out
+
+Do not re-check these; they were checked during the incident and came back clean.
+
+- **Vendor incident.** Their status page showed nothing for any failure window.
+- **Response body detail.** The `502`s carry no body. There is no vendor-side
+  error message to find; absence of one is expected for an edge timeout.
+
+## Before changing `BATCH`
+
+Raising it re-opens a question that cost a morning. The cheap decisive move is
+to measure, not to bisect again:
+
+1. **Record per-call wall time and record count** for every call in `sync.sh`
+   (`curl -w '%{time_total}'`). One week of this data answers every gap above
+   and costs nothing.
+2. Then set `BATCH` from the observed p99 duration with real margin, rather than
+   from the record-count limit in the vendor's docs.
+
+Do not raise `BATCH` on the argument that the docs allow 1000, or that 200
+"seems conservative" — neither produces a duration measurement, and reasoning
+about this without one is what cost the morning this file records. A vendor
+statement that the timeout has been raised is a reason to measure, not a reason
+to raise `BATCH` on its own: the timeout claim in this file is itself a single
+unverified verbal source, and a second one is no firmer than the first.
+
+## When this record goes stale
+
+The 200 is justified only while the premises hold. It stops being the right
+value if any of these change, and the reasoning here should be redone rather
+than patched:
+
+- The account moves off the shared tier, or the vendor removes or raises the
+  30-second gateway timeout.
+- `pack.py`'s payload shape changes such that per-record processing cost moves.
+- Per-call durations get measured, which supersedes this entire file.
